{
  "abstract": "Naik and Selman's Hypothesis B states that every polynomial-time function on binary strings computable with one nonadaptive batch of NP queries can also be computed with logarithmically many adaptive NP queries. We prove that this inclusion implies P = NP, answering their question about its consequence. Conversely, P = NP implies that the two function classes coincide. The proof uses affine hulls of verifier witnesses over the binary field to turn a polynomial-size list of candidate hulls into a deterministic witness search. Lean verifies both directions for total functions in an explicit binary register-machine model.",
